# Test: ze serial login -- authentication gate on serial console
# spec-appliance-login-shell AC-1..AC-9
#
# This test requires QEMU with serial port forwarding to exercise the full
# serial console login flow (gokrazy -> symlink -> ze login -> busybox).
# Unit tests in cmd/ze/login_test.go cover all login logic (argv[0] dispatch,
# valid/invalid credentials, fail-open, retry limits, non-terminal rejection,
# ZeFS fallback path).
#
# QEMU test plan (when appliance serial test infrastructure is ready):
#   1. Build appliance image with ze-serial-shell package
#   2. Boot in QEMU with -serial stdio
#   3. Send credentials via serial port
#   4. Verify shell access granted on correct password
#   5. Verify rejection on wrong password
#   6. Verify fail-open when ZeFS is missing
#
# The full interactive flow (credentials over a serial TTY) still needs the
# QEMU serial plan above. What IS assertable offline, end to end through the
# real user entry point, is the argv[0] shell-invocation gate itself: gokrazy
# symlinks ash/sh to ze (isShellInvocation, cmd/ze/login.go:42-44) and
# loginMain rejects a non-terminal stdin before any authentication
# (cmd/ze/login.go:47-49). A negative control proves normal ze dispatch is
# unaffected by the symlink gate.


cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture appliance/serial-login
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK
reject=stderr:pattern=FAIL:
# Non-terminal rejection must happen BEFORE authentication: if the gate is
# bypassed, loginMain falls through to the ZeFS fail-open path and prints
# "granting access without authentication" (cmd/ze/login.go:59) -- an exec
# failure alone would fake the right exit code (mutation-verified).
reject=stderr:pattern=granting access
